WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.768 --> 00:00:03.569
WILL: Gabe, Clarence
congratulations.

00:00:03.636 --> 00:00:06.205
You've made it to the final
round of this competition.

00:00:06.272 --> 00:00:07.807
Now we're sending
you back to your home

00:00:07.874 --> 00:00:11.610
forges where you will recreate
an iconic weapon from history.

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:16.115
Make no mistake, gentlemen,
this is a tough sword to make.

00:00:16.182 --> 00:00:19.085
The last time this was
issued as a final challenge,

00:00:19.152 --> 00:00:22.888
one of our competitors did
not meet the parameters.

00:00:22.988 --> 00:00:29.628
That weapon is the Viking sword.

00:00:29.728 --> 00:00:32.898
From the eighth to 11th century,
the Vikings were the scourge

00:00:32.998 --> 00:00:34.700
of the northern European coast.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:37.136
One of their most effective
tools for raiding and pillaging

00:00:37.236 --> 00:00:39.872
was a Viking sword, a
straight-edged iron sword with

00:00:39.938 --> 00:00:41.841
a rounded tip and double edge.

00:00:41.908 --> 00:00:43.375
The Viking sword
was built to break

00:00:43.442 --> 00:00:46.445
through an opponent's defenses
using big, slashing blows.

00:00:46.545 --> 00:00:49.415
Since the blade was heavy and
usually swung with one hand,

00:00:49.481 --> 00:00:51.617
a large pommel and hilt
were used to balance

00:00:51.684 --> 00:00:52.819
the weight of the blade.

00:00:52.886 --> 00:00:54.453
Viking swords were
often highly prized

00:00:54.553 --> 00:00:57.589
by their owners and given names
such as Battle Snake and Dog

00:00:57.690 --> 00:00:59.024
of the Helmet.

00:00:59.125 --> 00:01:03.796
One was featured in the
2007 epic film, "Beowulf."

00:01:03.896 --> 00:01:06.132
This is your redemption.

00:01:06.232 --> 00:01:09.302
This could be your last
chance to show the world

00:01:09.368 --> 00:01:11.671
what you're truly capable of.

00:01:11.771 --> 00:01:12.805
Good luck, bladesmiths.

00:01:12.905 --> 00:01:14.773
We'll see you in five days.

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:15.741
 Best of luck to you.

00:01:15.808 --> 00:01:17.077
 Best of luck, man.

00:01:20.312 --> 00:01:22.715
It's day one at my home forge.

00:01:22.816 --> 00:01:24.517
I suppose it gives
me advantages.

00:01:24.584 --> 00:01:26.919
I've never made a
Viking sword before.

00:01:26.986 --> 00:01:29.188
But I'm feeling confident.

00:01:29.288 --> 00:01:30.490
It's a coal forge.

00:01:30.557 --> 00:01:32.792
I made it years ago.

00:01:32.858 --> 00:01:34.560
This is going to be something.

00:01:34.627 --> 00:01:37.563
By the end of the day, I'd
like to have the sword at least

00:01:37.663 --> 00:01:38.832
partially forged--

00:01:38.898 --> 00:01:42.401
my point established, the
bevels forged into place.

00:01:42.501 --> 00:01:44.104
If I'm halfway down
the sword blade,

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:47.474
and I can finish forging
tomorrow, that would be ideal.

00:01:54.013 --> 00:01:55.614
GABE: We're here
at Dragonfly Forge.

00:01:55.715 --> 00:01:59.052
Today we'll be making the
billet out a few pieces

00:01:59.152 --> 00:02:04.290
of forge welded cable, which
we'll cut, twist, weld.

00:02:04.357 --> 00:02:06.592
The cable has about
200 strands each,

00:02:06.659 --> 00:02:09.662
so when the two are combined,
it's going to be about 400

00:02:09.729 --> 00:02:11.531
wires comprising the billet.

00:02:11.597 --> 00:02:14.600
It'll make a very,
very strong blade.

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:18.037
Hopefully, I can get
those two put together.

00:02:18.137 --> 00:02:20.673
That didn't quite
go how we wanted.

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:22.575
As I was forging the
two pieces of cable

00:02:22.675 --> 00:02:24.677
together, the first
heat, I couldn't

00:02:24.744 --> 00:02:26.245
get the forge weld to stick.

00:02:26.311 --> 00:02:27.647
I just can't get the sweet spot.

00:02:27.747 --> 00:02:29.081
I need it to be hot enough.

00:02:29.148 --> 00:02:30.349
It's not getting
a full combustion.

00:02:30.449 --> 00:02:31.651
And that's why
it's, like, so much

00:02:31.751 --> 00:02:34.620
gas that it can't all burn.

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:37.290
The next one, I got a little
hotter, and she stuck.

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:38.424
The welds look great.

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:44.430 line:0%
 Today I just want
to draw out this bar.

00:02:44.497 --> 00:02:46.499
I rethought some of the
things from yesterday,

00:02:46.566 --> 00:02:48.668
and I think I was
a little rushed.

00:02:48.768 --> 00:02:51.570
Today I'm going to take my time,
make sure I draw out that bar

00:02:51.637 --> 00:02:55.641
and get that metal kind of
slimmed down, thinner, longer.

00:02:55.741 --> 00:02:57.844
And that way, it'll
also be easier to heat.

00:03:02.615 --> 00:03:04.117
It's hot.

00:03:04.217 --> 00:03:05.551
I'm tired.

00:03:05.617 --> 00:03:08.387
I'm not used to being
tired from forging.

00:03:08.487 --> 00:03:11.557
A little bit worried
about running out of time.

00:03:11.657 --> 00:03:15.061
So it's hard to work when
you don't have the energy.

00:03:15.128 --> 00:03:17.463
I'm really worn out.

00:03:17.529 --> 00:03:20.599
I hope that I can
get through this.

00:03:20.699 --> 00:03:21.401
[bleep]

00:03:24.037 --> 00:03:26.072 line:0%
GABE: It's now the
start of day three.

00:03:26.139 --> 00:03:28.975
Really looking pretty
darn good at this stage.

00:03:29.041 --> 00:03:32.745
The quench is really the make
or break point of the blade.

00:03:32.812 --> 00:03:36.082
It's about ready for
the moment of truth.

00:03:36.182 --> 00:03:37.617
I cannot afford a failure.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:50.396
The absolute worst thing that
can happen during a quench

00:03:50.463 --> 00:03:52.264
is to have a fatal crack.

00:03:52.365 --> 00:03:55.301
There's no choice but to
start over at that point.

00:03:55.401 --> 00:03:57.769
Pretty damn straight.

00:03:57.836 --> 00:03:59.538
Looks good.

00:03:59.605 --> 00:04:04.644 line:0%
We're over the largest hump
of the whole project now.

00:04:04.711 --> 00:04:05.544 line:7%
CLARENCE: Day five.

00:04:05.645 --> 00:04:08.547 line:0%
The plan is to
finish everything,

00:04:08.647 --> 00:04:11.283
grind, put the
guard and the handle

00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:15.054
on, and quench the blade.

00:04:15.121 --> 00:04:16.656
Working a sword
like this by hand,

00:04:16.722 --> 00:04:19.324
especially it being the first
of these that I've done,

00:04:19.391 --> 00:04:21.027
there's a lot of difficulty.

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:28.334
The quench went all right.

00:04:28.401 --> 00:04:31.303
We got quite a bit
of bend in here.

00:04:31.403 --> 00:04:34.173
And I, of course,
don't want to snap it.

00:04:34.273 --> 00:04:36.709
This is really frightening.

00:04:36.809 --> 00:04:38.677
Got a lot of it out.

00:04:38.778 --> 00:04:41.180
Ready to finish.

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:43.449
Initially, I had a
different type of pommel

00:04:43.549 --> 00:04:45.218
set up that I wanted to do.

00:04:45.318 --> 00:04:47.654
I'm not going to have time,
so I have to improvise.

00:04:50.723 --> 00:04:55.495
Well, we made it
work, which is good.

00:04:58.698 --> 00:05:00.365 line:0%
GABE: It's now the
final day, and I

00:05:00.432 --> 00:05:02.568
have all the assembly to do.

00:05:02.635 --> 00:05:04.537
Although there's still
a lot to do today,

00:05:04.603 --> 00:05:06.605
I'm confident I can pull it off.

00:05:06.672 --> 00:05:10.409
The handle is a two-piece
hardwood core over the tang.

00:05:10.475 --> 00:05:11.944
And that's going to
be wrapped in leather.

00:05:12.045 --> 00:05:16.148
It's a very simple and
utilitarian handle design.

00:05:16.214 --> 00:05:18.984
OK, I think that did the trick.

00:05:19.051 --> 00:05:23.122
We only have a few hours left,
so I'm engraving the Roman

00:05:23.222 --> 00:05:25.624
numerals XIII on here.

00:05:25.724 --> 00:05:27.827
13 is a lucky
number in my family.

00:05:27.893 --> 00:05:30.496
It wouldn't hurt to have
a little extra luck.

00:05:30.562 --> 00:05:33.733
It's time to pack this thing up.

00:05:33.833 --> 00:05:36.468
DAVID: Gentlemen, to test
the sharpness of your blade,

00:05:36.535 --> 00:05:39.271
I'll be cutting into
our cargo net here.

00:05:39.338 --> 00:05:41.040
Now, Gabe, you're up first.

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:42.041
You ready for this?

00:05:42.141 --> 00:05:42.908
 Definitely.

00:05:43.008 --> 00:05:43.609
DAVID: All right.

00:05:52.085 --> 00:05:53.285
Well, Gabe, that
blade made pretty

00:05:53.352 --> 00:05:55.488
short work of that rope net.

00:05:55.555 --> 00:05:57.824
The weight overall
has a nice feel.

00:05:57.924 --> 00:05:59.391
Definitely did a good job.

00:05:59.458 --> 00:06:01.059
Definitely a sharp weapon.

00:06:01.126 --> 00:06:02.828
It felt good cutting.

00:06:02.928 --> 00:06:03.629
Nicely done, Gabe.

00:06:03.696 --> 00:06:04.697
 Thank you.

00:06:04.764 --> 00:06:06.198
 All right,
Clarence, your turn.

00:06:06.265 --> 00:06:07.132
You ready?
- I'm ready.

00:06:07.199 --> 00:06:07.900
DAVID: OK.

00:06:15.107 --> 00:06:16.876
All right, so, Clarence, a
couple of things I really

00:06:16.976 --> 00:06:19.445
like about this blade--
the weight is very nice,

00:06:19.512 --> 00:06:22.481
and the balance
is very well done.

00:06:22.548 --> 00:06:27.219
This big lip in the handle,
though, is really not

00:06:27.285 --> 00:06:29.421
very comfortable on the hand.

00:06:29.521 --> 00:06:32.691
But your blade did
well, cut nicely.

00:06:32.758 --> 00:06:34.293
Well done.

00:06:34.393 --> 00:06:36.462
DOUG: Bladesmiths,
the Viking sword,

00:06:36.562 --> 00:06:39.198
wielded by the hands of
the feared Viking warriors,

00:06:39.298 --> 00:06:41.066
forged for one purpose--

00:06:41.167 --> 00:06:42.268
kill the enemy.

00:06:42.334 --> 00:06:43.835
To see how lethal
your weapons are,

00:06:43.936 --> 00:06:45.972
I will take your Viking
sword and deliver killing

00:06:46.038 --> 00:06:47.639
blows to this ballistics dummy.

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:50.108
Let's see what kind of
damage your weapon can do.

00:06:50.175 --> 00:06:50.909
Gabe, you're up first.

00:06:50.976 --> 00:06:51.377
You ready?

00:06:51.477 --> 00:06:52.278
 I'm ready.

00:06:52.345 --> 00:06:52.945
DOUG: Let's do this.

00:07:05.023 --> 00:07:08.360
OK, Gabe, the handle is
comfortable with the wrapping

00:07:08.427 --> 00:07:09.094
that you did there.

00:07:09.195 --> 00:07:10.763
It feels good.

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:14.200
As you can see, it lacerated the
chest, the chest cavity here.

00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:16.502
It even took out some
bones of the ribs.

00:07:16.569 --> 00:07:19.572
It is sharp on both edges
and penetrated easily

00:07:19.672 --> 00:07:21.474
on this ballistics dummy.

00:07:21.540 --> 00:07:24.410
Most importantly, it will kill.

00:07:24.477 --> 00:07:25.243
Good job.

00:07:25.310 --> 00:07:26.211
 Thank you.

00:07:26.278 --> 00:07:27.213
 Clarence, you're up next.

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:27.979
Are you ready?

00:07:28.046 --> 00:07:28.781
 I'm ready.

00:07:38.423 --> 00:07:41.560
DOUG: OK, Clarence, it
feels good to wield.

00:07:41.626 --> 00:07:43.762
It's got great balance to it.

00:07:43.863 --> 00:07:48.567
The one thing that it
didn't do was cut cleanly

00:07:48.633 --> 00:07:50.102
through the ballistics dummy.

00:07:50.203 --> 00:07:51.470
It felt like it
was bouncing off.

00:07:51.537 --> 00:07:52.972
And I wonder if that's
your edge geometry here

00:07:53.038 --> 00:07:55.541
being thick and the
sharpness of your blade.

00:07:55.641 --> 00:07:59.979
But in the cuts that it did
do, the weight did penetrate,

00:08:00.045 --> 00:08:01.513
broke some bones.

00:08:01.613 --> 00:08:05.384
It penetrated on a
deep thrust coming out.

00:08:05.451 --> 00:08:08.253
And that would be a
very lethal wound.

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:10.622
And your weapon will kill.

00:08:10.689 --> 00:08:13.225
DAVID: Now the Viking
sword was commonly used

00:08:13.325 --> 00:08:14.694
in conjunction with a shield.

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:16.695
So to test the
strength of your sword,

00:08:16.795 --> 00:08:18.964
I'm going to take four blows
into these wooden shield

00:08:19.064 --> 00:08:22.434
edges, two with either
edge of your blade.

00:08:22.534 --> 00:08:23.402
Gabe, you're up first.

00:08:23.469 --> 00:08:24.369
You ready?
- Yes, sir.

00:08:24.469 --> 00:08:25.137
DAVID: All right.

00:08:37.883 --> 00:08:42.187
All right, so Gabe, still
in the same shape it was in.

00:08:42.254 --> 00:08:43.188
That's good.

00:08:43.255 --> 00:08:44.623
It's kept its straightness.

00:08:44.723 --> 00:08:46.792
It feels like your edge
held up really nicely.

00:08:46.859 --> 00:08:49.428
It definitely cut
into the shield good.

00:08:49.495 --> 00:08:50.195
Good job.

00:08:50.262 --> 00:08:51.330
 Thank you.

00:08:51.397 --> 00:08:53.165
 All right,
Clarence, you're up.

00:08:53.232 --> 00:08:54.032
You ready?
- I'm ready.

00:08:54.099 --> 00:08:54.767
DAVID: OK.

00:09:09.815 --> 00:09:13.586
Well, Clarence, your
edge held up nicely.

00:09:13.686 --> 00:09:16.421
That handle still--
ugh, in my hand,

00:09:16.521 --> 00:09:17.956
it's still a bit of an issue.

00:09:18.056 --> 00:09:22.494
But swinging it, the weight
and the balance is really nice.

00:09:22.594 --> 00:09:25.096
All in all, well done.

00:09:25.163 --> 00:09:26.666
 Thank you.

00:09:26.766 --> 00:09:31.704
WILL: Gabe, Clarence, your road
to redemption was a gauntlet.

00:09:31.771 --> 00:09:34.773
The judges have deliberated, and
they've made a final decision.

00:09:34.873 --> 00:09:37.109
The "Forged in
Fire" champion is--

00:09:44.016 --> 00:09:45.017
Gabe.

00:09:45.084 --> 00:09:46.719
Congratulations.

00:09:46.785 --> 00:09:48.988
Clarence, please
surrender your weapon.

00:09:51.490 --> 00:09:53.258
CLARENCE: The first
time I competed,

00:09:53.325 --> 00:09:55.861
I wound up out in round one.

00:09:55.928 --> 00:09:58.197
This time, I've made it
through to the finale.

00:09:58.297 --> 00:10:00.899
So I may not be a "Forged
in Fire" champion,

00:10:00.966 --> 00:10:04.336
but I have proven
to myself that I can

00:10:04.436 --> 00:10:06.204
go much further than I thought.

00:10:06.271 --> 00:10:11.176
So there'll be definitely
lots more to look forward to.

00:10:11.243 --> 00:10:13.379
 Gabe, congratulations.

00:10:13.446 --> 00:10:15.548
You are the "Forged
in Fire" champion

00:10:15.648 --> 00:10:17.516
and will be receiving
that check for 10 grand.

00:10:17.583 --> 00:10:21.086
[applause]

00:10:21.153 --> 00:10:23.221
If that's not redemption, I
don't know what is, brother.

00:10:23.321 --> 00:10:25.624
 Feels so sweet after all that.

00:10:25.690 --> 00:10:27.559
 I love the dimensions
of that blade.

00:10:27.626 --> 00:10:28.961
It's a great cutter.

00:10:29.028 --> 00:10:30.296
When I was going through
that rope, it just went--

00:10:30.396 --> 00:10:31.730
[imitates swishing]
--right through.

00:10:31.830 --> 00:10:34.199
You definitely know how to
put an edge on something.

00:10:34.266 --> 00:10:36.568
 I feel like I've
completely redeemed myself.

00:10:36.635 --> 00:10:38.137
It feels incredible.

00:10:38.237 --> 00:10:41.140
Like, I never thought that the
title "Forged in Fire" champion

00:10:41.240 --> 00:10:43.275
would mean as much as
it does to me right now.

00:10:43.342 --> 00:10:46.979
[music playing]

